f 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441930 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441930 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441930 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441930 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441930 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441930 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441930 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fec331a1-3371-57b3-ae6b-a96dcb6d5ba8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441931 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8c/8cc4feb2e391a0a25bb531592205dbc738f0fd0582fb2d56a1627a8584a42618.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4a1842a-2984-5d5e-9da6-07b14124ecae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441931 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{12}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{17})}^{2}+{(\\frac{12}{17})}^{2}}≈0.73\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441932 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441932 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e3/e329b7c1fdb9652affd6381c5002efe4c8eed6fa2b13da43c9a2a6116daa386b.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b46ee022-1c34-5022-81e0-9eb66a5f128b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441932 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=5I+1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{29},$ $\\beta =\\frac{30}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{12}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{30}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{12}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{30}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{29},\\,\\beta =\\frac{30}{29}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{12}{29}+\\frac{30}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441933 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441933 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441933 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441933 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/da02d808aac69b1b7381f6e6ca7a006b863e6b808a89ecc7a4f79be0db7556b9.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441933 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d642175b-da68-5c1f-a969-cd59bcc47e2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441933 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $24\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=24I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441934 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441934 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441934 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a84a1501854f6efb988b7c02c3a1b5a80b80dbf3d5fc7ce94406778cc6dcadb5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d671cb3b-5175-53db-90fa-e1b9c7534825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441934 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=10I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441935 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441935 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441935 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441935 #edb/ref 1071 "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441935 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e14b31da-25df-5fa5-9bfc-4a5fa3c83d9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441935 #edb/ref 1074 "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=40I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
